{"name":"Vaquero Boots","hex":"#855f43","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vaquero","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cowboy; a herdsman."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vaquero"},{"term":"boots","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of boot"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A condom."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/boots"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vaquero","blocks":[{"text":"The vaquero is a horse-mounted livestock herder of a tradition that has its roots in the Iberian Peninsula, and extensively developed in what is today Mexico from methods brought to the Americas from Spain. The vaquero became the foundation for the North American cowboy, in Northern Mexico, Southwestern United States, Florida and Western Canada."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Vaqueros.jpg/330px-Vaqueros.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaquero","wikidata":"Q1401609"},{"title":"Boot","blocks":[{"text":"A boot is a type of footwear. Most boots mainly cover the foot and the ankle, while some also cover some part of the lower calf. Some boots extend up the leg, sometimes as far as the knee or even the hip. Most boots have a heel that is clearly distinguishable from the rest of the sole, even if the two are made of one piece. Traditionally made of leather or rubber, modern boots are made from a variety of materials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cliff_Climbers_Combat_boot_Shoes_Blast.png/330px-Cliff_Climbers_Combat_boot_Shoes_Blast.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boot","wikidata":"Q190868"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Music_of_the_Plains_%28mural_study%2C_Kilgore%2C_Texas_Post_Office%29_SAAM-1974.28.333_1.jpg/500px-Music_of_the_Plains_%28mural_study%2C_Kilgore%2C_Texas_Post_Office%29_SAAM-1974.28.333_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Music_of_the_Plains_%28mural_study%2C_Kilgore%2C_Texas_Post_Office%29_SAAM-1974.28.333_1.jpg/1280px-Music_of_the_Plains_%28mural_study%2C_Kilgore%2C_Texas_Post_Office%29_SAAM-1974.28.333_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Music_of_the_Plains_%28mural_study%2C_Kilgore%2C_Texas_Post_Office%29_SAAM-1974.28.333_1.jpg/500px-Music_of_the_Plains_%28mural_study%2C_Kilgore%2C_Texas_Post_Office%29_SAAM-1974.28.333_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Music of the Plains (mural study, Kilgore, Texas, 1939) by Xavier Gonzalez. This New Deal-era artwork features a vaquero serenading a woman, symbolizing the deep Hispanic cultural roots in Kilgore's identity. The original study is housed in the Smithsonian American Art Museum.[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMusic_of_the_Plains_(mural_study%2C_Kilgore%2C_Texas_Post_Office)_SAAM-1974.28.333_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Hackamore_equipment.jpg/500px-Hackamore_equipment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Hackamore_equipment.jpg/1280px-Hackamore_equipment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Hackamore_equipment.jpg/500px-Hackamore_equipment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Classic vaquero-style hackamore equipment. Horsehair mecates top row, rawhide bosals in second row with other equipment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHackamore_equipment.jpg"},{"srcset"